Sir Calder: You can hear it too, right? The flicker.
- The lights?
	Nobita: The lights?
	Sir Calder: No. Not the lights. The answers.
	Sir Calder: We stopped checking. We just reacted to what sounded right.
	Sir Calder: Confidence felt like correctness. Half truths were enough. Look at this P ∧ Q. Both must stand. If one collapses, the whole thing falls.
	- So we ignored the truth?
		Nobita: So we ignored the truth?
		Sir Calder: Exactly. That’s how the first cracks appeared. People followed what sounded right, not what was true.
		Sir Calder: We reacted before we evaluated. That is how contradiction spreads. => END
	- I… see now.
